Before oil, Dubai was a modest pearl‑diving and fishing port whose economy rose and fell with the Gulf’s pearl trade, then stagnated after that trade collapsed in the 1930s. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Dubai)

## Pre‑oil economic pillars

Dubai’s economy before the 1960s rested on three main activities:

- **Pearl diving and trading** – For much of the 19th and early 20th centuries, pearling was the dominant industry, making Dubai a key regional center for collecting, selling, and exporting pearls. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dubai)
- **Fishing and boat‑building** – Alongside pearling, fishing provided daily subsistence and local trade, supported by a small fleet of wooden dhows and associated crafts. [frommers](https://www.frommers.com/destinations/dubai/in-depth/history/)
- **Maritime trade via Dubai Creek** – The natural harbor of Dubai Creek enabled trade links with India, Persia, and East Africa; by the late 19th century Dubai had become an important Gulf port with large souks. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dubai)

Traditional inland activities such as **date cultivation and small‑scale herding** also existed, but the coastal economy centered on the sea. [frommers](https://www.frommers.com/destinations/dubai/in-depth/history/)

## Boom, collapse, and hardship

- **Late 1800s–1920s:** With a thriving pearl industry, Dubai prospered and was described as the main trading port on the Gulf coast. [frommers](https://www.frommers.com/destinations/dubai/in-depth/history/)
- **1930s onward:** The pearling industry collapsed due to the Great Depression, World War I disruptions, and especially the rise of Japanese cultured pearls, which undercut natural Gulf pearls. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Dubai)
- **Consequences:** The collapse plunged Dubai into a deep economic depression; many residents lived in poverty or migrated to other parts of the Gulf. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Dubai)

## What Dubai looked like in the 1950s–early 1960s

By the 1950s, Dubai was still a **small trading and fishing post**:

- **Population:** Roughly under 20,000 (some sources cite around 5,000–20,000 depending on decade and definition). [frommers](https://www.frommers.com/destinations/dubai/in-depth/history/)
- **Infrastructure:** Unpaved roads, limited electricity and clean water, few schools and clinics; most housing was simple “barasti” homes made from palm fronds and other local materials. [onlybyland](https://onlybyland.com/dubai-before-and-after/)
- **Transport and daily life:** Movement was by foot, camel, or boat; life followed the seasonal rhythm of pearl diving and fishing. [dxbnewsnetwork](https://www.dxbnewsnetwork.com/life-in-dubai-before-oil-how-it-all-started)
- **Economy:** Still based on fishing, residual pearl activity, and regional trade, but far from wealthy. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Dubai)

Even before oil revenues arrived, Dubai’s rulers began investing in basic infrastructure (e.g., dredging the Creek in the late 1950s) using loans from local merchants and neighboring rulers, aiming to strengthen its role as a trading hub. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dubai)

## Why this matters as a baseline

This pre‑oil picture—small population, pearl‑dependent economy, minimal infrastructure, and vulnerability to external shocks—forms the stark contrast to the rapid, oil‑fueled transformation that began after oil was discovered in the late 1960s and exports started in 1969. [dxbnewsnetwork](https://www.dxbnewsnetwork.com/life-in-dubai-before-oil-how-it-all-started)



Dubai’s oil was discovered in **1966** at the offshore **Fateh field**, and the **first oil exports** left Dubai on **22 September 1969**. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fateh_Oil_Field)

## Exact dates and key figures

- **Oil discovery (Fateh field):**  
  - **1966** is the universally cited discovery year.  
  - Some sources give a specific date of **6 June 1966** for the initial discovery by the Dubai Petroleum Company (a Conoco subsidiary), with a second producing zone announced on **21 June 1966**. [belfercenter](https://www.belfercenter.org/sites/default/files/pantheon_files/files/publication/Ramos_-_Working_Paper_-_FINAL.pdf)
  - Other technical sources list **23 June 1966** as the discovery date for Fateh. [ports.marinelink](https://ports.marinelink.com/oilrigs/rig/fateh)
  - The well that confirmed the field, **Fateh‑1**, was drilled by Continental Oil Company (Conoco) and came in at an estimated rate of around **100,000 barrels per day**. [aapg](https://www.aapg.org/news-and-media/details/explorer/articleid/66523)

- **First production and exports:**  
  - **Production start:** Officially began in **1969**, with some accounts specifying **6 September 1969** when the ruler opened a valve on an offshore platform to allow the first flow. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fateh_Oil_Field)
  - **First export shipment:** The first cargo of crude was shipped to world markets on **22 September 1969**. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fateh_Oil_Field)
  - **Size of first shipment:** The initial export cargo was about **180,000 barrels**, sent to Conoco refineries in the UK. [belfercenter](https://www.belfercenter.org/sites/default/files/pantheon_files/files/publication/Ramos_-_Working_Paper_-_FINAL.pdf)

- **Early output and revenue (context):**  
  - By the end of the first full year of exports, Dubai’s oil fields had produced roughly **3.56 million barrels**, generating about **$376,000** in revenue; by the end of **1970**, revenue had risen to around **$11.6 million**. [belfercenter](https://www.belfercenter.org/sites/default/files/pantheon_files/files/publication/Ramos_-_Working_Paper_-_FINAL.pdf)

So the “founding moment” of Dubai’s oil era is: **discovery in 1966 (Fateh field), first exports on 22 September 1969 (≈180,000 barrels).** [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fateh_Oil_Field)


Sheikh Rashid bin Saeed Al Maktoum (ruler of Dubai from 1958 to 1990) is widely regarded as the architect of modern Dubai, using oil revenues and shrewd trade policy to turn a small creek‑side port into a diversified commercial hub. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rashid_bin_Saeed_Al_Maktoum)

## Core development philosophy

Rashid’s guiding idea was **diversification and long‑term planning**, explicitly anticipating that oil would not last forever.  He is famous for the quote: “My grandfather rode a camel, my father rode a camel, I drive a Mercedes, my son drives a Land Rover, his son will drive a Land Rover, but his son will ride a camel,” meaning Dubai must prepare for a post‑oil future. [gulfnews](https://gulfnews.com/uae/remembering-sheikh-rashid-bin-saeed-the-visionary-who-shaped-dubais-future-1.500296983)

His strategy combined:

- **Trade‑first orientation** – treating Dubai primarily as a regional entrepôt and logistics hub rather than just an oil state. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rashid_bin_Saeed_Al_Maktoum)
- **State‑led infrastructure investment** – using oil income and borrowed capital to build ports, airports, roads, and utilities that would attract merchants and shipping lines. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rashid_bin_Saeed_Al_Maktoum)
- **Light taxation and free‑port policies** – lowering duties and keeping regulation business‑friendly to draw traders away from more taxed ports. [christopherdavidson](http://www.christopherdavidson.net/files/DVOS_chapter3.pdf)

## Key policies and decisions

### 1. Free‑port and trade policy

- Early in his rule, Rashid **formalized Dubai’s status as a free port**, building on earlier traditions of low duties and minimal interference in commerce. [christopherdavidson](http://www.christopherdavidson.net/files/DVOS_chapter3.pdf)
- He **cut or exempted many tariffs** and kept administrative barriers low, making Dubai the “safest and most profitable free port” in the Gulf for regional merchants. [christopherdavidson](http://www.christopherdavidson.net/files/DVOS_chapter3.pdf)
- This policy deliberately targeted **Indian, Iranian, and other regional traders**, encouraging them to use Dubai as their main transshipment and re‑export base. [christopherdavidson](http://www.christopherdavidson.net/files/DVOS_chapter3.pdf)

### 2. Pre‑oil infrastructure bets (before and just after discovery)

Even before oil revenues were substantial, Rashid committed to large infrastructure projects, often financed by loans:

- **Dubai Creek dredging (1958–1959):** He commissioned a British consultancy (Halcrow) to design a plan to deepen and widen the Creek, then raised funds via “creek bonds” and a **£500,000 loan from Kuwait** to execute the dredging.  This turned a shallow, silting waterway into a viable harbor for larger dhows and small steamers. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rashid_bin_Saeed_Al_Maktoum)
- **First structured town plan:** He hired British consultant **John Harris** to produce Dubai’s first formal town plan, a rare move in the Gulf at the time, laying out roads, zoning, and public services. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rashid_bin_Saeed_Al_Maktoum)
- **Maktoum Bridge (1963):** He financed the first bridge across the Creek (with a loan from Qatar’s ruler), physically linking Bur Dubai and Deira and enabling road‑based commerce. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rashid_bin_Saeed_Al_Maktoum)
- **Dubai International Airport (1960):** He oversaw the official inauguration of the airport, positioning Dubai for air cargo and passenger traffic well before oil wealth peaked. [gulfnews](https://gulfnews.com/uae/remembering-sheikh-rashid-bin-saeed-the-visionary-who-shaped-dubais-future-1.500296983)

### 3. Using oil revenues strategically (post‑1969)

After oil exports began in 1969, Rashid directed revenues into **trade‑enabling infrastructure** rather than just consumption:

- **Port Rashid (early 1970s):** He ordered construction of a four‑berth deep‑sea container terminal (Port Rashid), explicitly to handle growing cargo volumes and relieve the Creek. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rashid_bin_Saeed_Al_Maktoum)
- **Jebel Ali Port (foundation 1976, partial operations 1979):** He laid the groundwork for what would become one of the world’s largest container ports, again betting on Dubai as a global logistics node. [gulfnews](https://gulfnews.com/uae/remembering-sheikh-rashid-bin-saeed-the-visionary-who-shaped-dubais-future-1.500296983)
- **Dubai World Trade Centre (1979):** He built the 39‑storey tower, then the tallest in the Middle East, to host exhibitions and attract international business. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rashid_bin_Saeed_Al_Maktoum)
- **Dubai Dry Docks (1983):** He opened large ship‑repair facilities capable of handling vessels up to one million tons, further cementing Dubai’s maritime role. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rashid_bin_Saeed_Al_Maktoum)
- **Industrial and utility projects:** Under his rule Dubai also launched major projects such as **DUBAL (aluminium smelter)**, desalination plants, and expanded power and water networks, all supporting industry and population growth. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rashid_bin_Saeed_Al_Maktoum)

### 4. Institutional and social foundations

- **Municipal council (1958):** Rashid established a municipal council to manage hygiene, sanitation, and urban planning, later evolving into a full municipality that coordinated development. [gulfnews](https://gulfnews.com/preview/story/TpbnHNJvApW4wfNDnDNIYrQ1BYN7BjLCeNoMaySrKgLLT9fLfPrrifn0Gamm3x8C)
- **National Bank of Dubai (1963):** He supported the creation of the first national bank, providing local financing for trade and development. [gulfnews](https://gulfnews.com/uae/remembering-sheikh-rashid-bin-saeed-the-visionary-who-shaped-dubais-future-1.500296983)
- **Social spending from oil:** After 1966–69, he channeled oil income into **education, health, and social security**, improving living standards and building a more stable workforce. [gulfnews](https://gulfnews.com/uae/remembering-sheikh-rashid-bin-saeed-the-visionary-who-shaped-dubais-future-1.500296983)

## Why his decisions mattered

Rashid’s policies created a **self‑reinforcing cycle**: low taxes and good ports/airports attracted traders; trade generated fees and jobs; oil money funded more infrastructure; more infrastructure attracted even more trade and investment.  This is why modern Dubai’s economy is so heavily oriented toward logistics, finance, tourism, and real estate rather than relying mainly on hydrocarbons. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rashid_bin_Saeed_Al_Maktoum)


Jebel Ali Port was built between **1976 and 1979** as a bold, debt‑financed bet by Sheikh Rashid bin Saeed Al Maktoum to turn Dubai into a global shipping hub; the original construction cost is not precisely published in open sources, but later expansion phases have well‑documented budgets in the **hundreds of millions to billions of dollars**. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_of_Jebel_Ali)

## Original construction (1976–1979)

- **Start of construction:** Work on the port began in **1976**. [ship-technology](https://www.ship-technology.com/projects/jebel-ali-port-dubai/)
- **Inauguration:** The port was officially opened and inaugurated by **Queen Elizabeth II on 26 February 1979**. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_of_Jebel_Ali)
- **Purpose:** It was built to supplement the smaller Port Rashid and to handle much larger container ships and cargo volumes, with an attached free‑zone concept already in mind. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_of_Jebel_Ali)
- **Cost:** Contemporary sources and summaries do not give a single, precise figure for the **total 1976–79 construction cost**; they describe it as a very large, “adventurous” project for a small emirate, financed largely through state resources and borrowing. [wpassets.porttechnology](https://wpassets.porttechnology.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/05/25183954/PT41-14.pdf)

## Later expansions with published costs

Because the original cost is not clearly stated, the best concrete numbers come from later phases:

- **2000s masterplan (to 2020):**  
  - A major expansion program to raise container capacity to about **21.8 million TEU** by 2020 was budgeted at around **AED 4.2 billion (≈US$1.1 billion)**. [meed](https://www.meed.com/shortlist-prepared-for-jebel-ali-port-masterplan/)
  - This plan was implemented in multiple phases, including deepening channels, adding berths, and building new terminals. [meed](https://www.meed.com/shortlist-prepared-for-jebel-ali-port-masterplan/)

- **Terminal 2 (T2) expansion (mid‑2000s):**  
  - The whole T2 expansion (multiple phases) was estimated at about **US$1.5 billion**, spread over more than 1.4 million m². [ship-technology](https://www.ship-technology.com/projects/jebel-ali-port-dubai/)
  - The first stage of T2 alone was reported at roughly **US$1.4 billion (AED ~5 billion)**. [freightwaves](https://www.freightwaves.com/news/dubai-ports-starts-jebel-ali-expansion-project)
  - Dredging and reclamation contracts for T2 were awarded at around **AED 1.85 billion** for key packages. [gulfnews](https://gulfnews.com/business/dp-world-awards-dh185b-container-terminal-2-contract-1.304752)

- **Terminal 3 and later projects:**  
  - A planned third terminal (T3) was estimated at about **US$850 million** for construction, with some broader plans for T3/reclaimed‑land terminal reaching **US$1.5 billion** before being delayed. [ship-technology](https://www.ship-technology.com/projects/jebel-ali-port-dubai/)
  - A new **Terminal 4 (T4)** project launched in 2015 was valued at about **US$1.6 billion**, adding roughly 3.1 million TEU of capacity by 2018. [marinelink](https://www.marinelink.com/news/container-terminal-begins395362)
  - In **2023**, DP World announced a further **US$1.3 billion** investment program for Jebel Ali Port and the Jebel Ali Free Zone. [ports.marinelink](https://ports.marinelink.com/ports/port/point-wells/news/dp-world-commits-1-3-billion-jebel-ali-port-jafza-expansion)

## Why the original cost matters

Even without a single published figure for 1976–79, the pattern is clear: Sheikh Rashid committed **very large, multi‑hundred‑million‑dollar‑scale infrastructure spending** at a time when Dubai’s population and oil income were still modest, betting that a deep‑water port plus free‑zone would generate far more long‑term revenue than oil alone. [en.wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_of_Jebel_Ali)

Dubai’s economy is now overwhelmingly non‑oil: **over 95% of Dubai’s GDP comes from non‑oil sectors**, with hydrocarbons accounting for **well under 5%** (often cited as **less than 1–2%** in recent years). [timesofdubai](https://timesofdubai.ae/how-diversification-shapes-dubais-non-oil-economy/)

## Current oil share of Dubai’s GDP

- Dubai’s Public Debt Management Office states that the emirate’s GDP is diversified across about **20 economic categories**, with **over 95% non‑oil based**. [timesofdubai](https://timesofdubai.ae/how-diversification-shapes-dubais-non-oil-economy/)
- Multiple summaries of official data note that **oil now contributes less than 5% of Dubai’s GDP**, and some analyses put the figure at **under 1%** when isolating crude oil specifically. [themiddleeastinsider](https://themiddleeastinsider.com/2026/03/01/uae-economy-guide/)
- The most recent official GDP tables do not break out “oil” as a separate line for Dubai; instead, hydrocarbons sit inside a small **“mining and quarrying”** category that was about **2.3% of Dubai’s GDP in early 2025**, and that category includes more than just crude oil. [siliconcanals](https://siliconcanals.com/t-dubai-oil-gdp-tourism-trade-aviation-economy/)

So the key point is: **Dubai’s economy today is >95% non‑oil, with oil/hydrocarbons a small single‑digit share and likely around 1–2% or less.** [timesofdubai](https://timesofdubai.ae/how-diversification-shapes-dubais-non-oil-economy/)

## How this compares to the UAE as a whole

For context, the **entire UAE** (dominated by Abu Dhabi’s oil fields) is still more hydrocarbon‑dependent than Dubai:

- Non‑oil sectors accounted for about **79.4% of UAE GDP in Q1 2026**, meaning oil and gas still make up roughly **20%** of the national economy. [gulfnews](https://gulfnews.com/amp/story/business%2Feconomy%2Fuae-gdp-grows-3-to-dh485-billion-in-first-quarter-of-2026-1.500630054)
- By contrast, **Dubai alone** is at **>95% non‑oil**, showing how far it has moved away from hydrocarbons compared with the federation average. [timesofdubai](https://timesofdubai.ae/how-diversification-shapes-dubais-non-oil-economy/)

## Main non‑oil sectors in Dubai

Dubai’s GDP is now driven by:

- **Trade and logistics** (wholesale/retail, re‑exports, Jebel Ali Port)  
- **Tourism, hospitality, and aviation** (airlines, airports, hotels, events)  
- **Real estate and construction**  
- **Financial and business services**  
- **Manufacturing and industry** (including aluminium, food processing, light manufacturing) [timesofdubai](https://timesofdubai.ae/how-diversification-shapes-dubais-non-oil-economy/)

This is the “twist” in Dubai’s story: it used oil to build infrastructure, but today **oil is a minor contributor to its GDP**, while trade, tourism, finance, and real estate dominate. [timesofdubai](https://timesofdubai.ae/how-diversification-shapes-dubais-non-oil-economy/)


Dubai’s free‑zone model—especially the **Dubai Airport Free Zone (DAFZA)**—is built on a set of deliberate regulatory and tax choices: **100% foreign ownership, full profit repatriation, customs and corporate‑tax advantages, and a “one‑stop” licensing regime** that sits partly outside standard federal commercial law. [moet.gov](https://www.moet.gov.ae/documents/20121/416990/DAFZA+ENg.pdf/516738b1-ed3e-712f-3034-c9bfd27ba412?t=1631908158168)

## Legal foundation of Dubai’s free zones

Dubai’s free zones rest on two layers of law:

- **Federal level:**  
  - **Federal Law No. 8 of 2004** on Financial Free Zones allows the UAE to create zones where most federal civil and commercial laws do not apply, giving them their own regulatory frameworks (criminal law still applies). [dfsa](https://www.dfsa.ae/your-resources/regulatory/laws-and-rules)
  - This law enabled zones like the **Dubai International Financial Centre (DIFC)** and set the template for other special zones. [dfsa](https://www.dfsa.ae/your-resources/regulatory/laws-and-rules)

- **Dubai level:**  
  - Each free zone is created by a **Dubai law or decree** that defines its boundaries, authority, and powers.  
  - For DAFZA specifically: **Law No. 2 of 1996** established the **Free Trade Zone in Dubai International Airport**, and later **Law No. 25 of 2009** reorganized the **Dubai Airport Freezone Authority (DAFZA)** as an autonomous government entity. [moet.gov](https://www.moet.gov.ae/documents/20121/416990/DAFZA+ENg.pdf/516738b1-ed3e-712f-3034-c9bfd27ba412?t=1631908158168)

This structure lets free zones offer **custom rules for licensing, ownership, and dispute resolution** that differ from mainland UAE. [dfsa](https://www.dfsa.ae/your-resources/regulatory/laws-and-rules)

## DAFZA: establishment and policy goals

- **Year established:** **1996**, under **Law No. 2 of 1996**. [moet.gov](https://www.moet.gov.ae/documents/20121/416990/DAFZA+ENg.pdf/516738b1-ed3e-712f-3034-c9bfd27ba412?t=1631908158168)
- **Location:** Inside the boundaries of **Dubai International Airport (DXB)**, explicitly to link international air cargo and passenger flows with business setup. [moet.gov](https://www.moet.gov.ae/documents/20121/416990/DAFZA+ENg.pdf/516738b1-ed3e-712f-3034-c9bfd27ba412?t)
- **Strategic aim:** Part of Dubai’s broader shift to an **investment‑ and trade‑driven economy**, using the airport’s connectivity to attract multinationals, logistics firms, and high‑value services. [uppersetup](https://www.uppersetup.com/en/blog/dafza-the-free-zone-at-dubai-airport-who-it-suits-how-it-is)

## Core incentives that attracted global business

DAFZA’s rules (typical of Dubai’s major free zones) include:

### 1. 100% foreign ownership and control

- From its launch in 1996, DAFZA allowed **100% foreign ownership** of companies, with no requirement for a local Emirati partner. [velmontcrest](https://velmontcrest.ae/insights/dubai-airport-free-zone-dafza-guide/)
- Foreign investors can hold all shares and act as directors/managers, removing the traditional “51% local sponsor” constraint that applied on the mainland until recent reforms. [velmontcrest](https://velmontcrest.ae/insights/dubai-airport-free-zone-dafza-guide/)

### 2. Tax and customs advantages

- **Corporate tax:**  
  - Under the UAE’s new federal corporate tax regime (**Federal Decree‑Law No. 47 of 2022** and related cabinet decisions), free‑zone entities that meet certain conditions can qualify as a **Qualifying Free Zone Person (QFZP)** and pay **0% corporate tax on qualifying income**, with **9%** on non‑qualifying income above a threshold. [velmontcrest](https://velmontcrest.ae/insights/dubai-airport-free-zone-dafza-guide/)
  - DAFZA companies can structure activities to benefit from this 0% rate on eligible income, which is a major draw for regional headquarters and trading operations. [velmontcrest](https://velmontcrest.ae/insights/dubai-airport-free-zone-dafza-guide/)

- **Customs and duties:**  
  - Free zones like DAFZA offer **customs duty exemptions or reductions** on imports/exports handled within the zone, plus streamlined customs procedures linked to the airport. [uppersetup](https://www.uppersetup.com/en/blog/dafza-the-free-zone-at-dubai-airport-who-it-suits-how-it-is)
  - This is especially valuable for air‑freight‑intensive businesses (electronics, pharma, luxury goods, aerospace parts). [uppersetup](https://www.uppersetup.com/en/blog/dafza-the-free-zone-at-dubai-airport-who-it-suits-how-it-is)

- **Profit and capital repatriation:**  
  - Companies in DAFZA can **repatriate 100% of profits and capital** with no restrictions, a key selling point for multinationals concerned about currency controls. [uppersetup](https://www.uppersetup.com/en/blog/dafza-the-free-zone-at-dubai-airport-who-it-suits-how-it-is)

### 3. One‑stop licensing and light regulation

- DAFZA operates as a **single licensing authority**: it issues trade, service, and industrial licenses; handles visas; and provides many government services in one place. [uppersetup](https://www.uppersetup.com/en/blog/dafza-the-free-zone-at-dubai-airport-who-it-suits-how-it-is)
- The zone offers **fast setup times**, standardized lease contracts, and ready‑made office/warehouse options, reducing administrative friction for foreign firms. [uppersetup](https://www.uppersetup.com/en/blog/dafza-the-free-zone-at-dubai-airport-who-it-suits-how-it-is)

### 4. Sector focus and clustering

- DAFZA was designed to attract **aviation‑linked, logistics, trading, and high‑value services** firms, leveraging proximity to DXB. [uppersetup](https://www.uppersetup.com/en/blog/dafza-the-free-zone-at-dubai-airport-who-it-suits-how-it-is)
- Over time it has hosted global brands in **aerospace, luxury goods, technology, and professional services**, creating a cluster effect that reinforces its appeal. [uppersetup](https://www.uppersetup.com/en/blog/dafza-the-free-zone-at-dubai-airport-who-it-suits-how-it-is)

## How DAFZA fits into Dubai’s wider free‑zone strategy

DAFZA was the **second free zone in Dubai** (after Jebel Ali Free Zone, JAFZA, created in the mid‑1980s) and the **first located at the airport**.  Together with JAFZA, DIFC, DMCC, and others, it forms a network where: [superbrands.s3.amazonaws](http://superbrands.s3.amazonaws.com/AAA%20MASTER%202%20PAGE%20PDF%20Case%20Studies/UAE/UAE%20Edition%2012/UAE%20Edition%2012%20Dafza.pdf)

- Each zone has **its own authority and rules**, but all share the core promises of **full foreign ownership, tax/customs benefits, and simplified administration**. [velmontcrest](https://velmontcrest.ae/insights/dubai-airport-free-zone-dafza-guide/)
- In **2015**, Dubai created a **Free Zone Council** to coordinate policy across zones, and in **2016** passed **Law No. 15 of 2016** to standardize certain regulatory aspects while preserving zone‑level flexibility. [dlp.dubai.gov](https://dlp.dubai.gov.ae/Legislation%20Reference/2016/Law%20No.%20(15)%20of%202016%20Concerning%20the%20Regulatory%20Legislation%20Issued%20by%20Free%20Zone.pdf)

These choices—especially the **early move to 100% foreign ownership, guaranteed profit repatriation, and later the 0% QFZP corporate‑tax option**—are the regulatory/tax decisions that made DAFZA and Dubai’s free zones attractive to global business. [moet.gov](https://www.moet.gov.ae/documents/20121/416990/DAFZA+ENg.pdf/516738b1-ed3e-712f-3034-c9bfd27ba412?t=1631908158168)